<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; font-size: 28px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Our Traditional Style of Dress – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 16px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 70%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 4th Grade (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Our Traditional Style of Dress</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> She Is Wearing a Pollera.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Extract specific information connected to time, quantities, and location.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can pick out details about time, quantities, and location in talks about clothing (e.g., 'Women wear this dress at festivals.'). Can respond to and clarify oral questions, in group activities, about traditional clothing (e.g., 'What is she wearing?' 'She is wearing a dress.'). Can ask and answer questions about where and when clothing is worn (e.g., 'Where do people wear this?' 'At festivals.').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f0f8ff;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 35%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 15%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays various pictures of people wearing different traditional Panamanian clothing, including a pollera. The teacher points to a picture of a pollera and says, "Look! She is wearing a pollera. It is beautiful traditional clothing. Women wear it for celebrations." The teacher uses gestures to indicate 'wearing' and 'beautiful'. Students listen and point to the pictures as the teacher names them.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher slowly repeats, "She is wearing a pollera. This is traditional attire. It is beautiful." The teacher models the pronunciation of key vocabulary like 'wearing' (/wɛrɪŋ/) and 'traditional' and highlights the /w/ sound. The teacher also models blending sounds for words like /dr/ /ɛs/ to form 'dress' and /pr-ɪ-t-i/ for 'pretty' by saying them clearly and slowly.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is she wearing a modern dress or traditional clothing?" (Expected answer: Traditional clothing.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is the pollera ugly or beautiful?" (Expected answer: Beautiful.)</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Short audio description about a girl wearing a pollera at a community event. The audio describes what she is wearing, where she is, and when she wears it (e.g., "Hi, my name is Sofia. I am at a big celebration. I am wearing a beautiful pollera and special jewelry. People wear this traditional attire during important events. My mom sewed it for me. I wear it in my community for festivals.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Listening: Comprehending short, simple oral descriptions about traditional clothing, extracting details about time, quantities, and location, and explaining cultural significance.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (Listening Focus):</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> The teacher plays the audio once. Students listen. "What is Sofia talking about?" (Expected answer: Her dress / traditional clothing.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> The teacher plays the audio a second time. Students listen and raise their hand for "True" or "False" after each statement.
                        <ul style="list-style-type: circle; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Sofia is wearing a t-shirt." (False)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"She is at a party/celebration." (True)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"She wears it during work." (False)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Her mom folded her dress." (False - mom sewed it)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Listening for specific details (time, quantity, location) about traditional clothing using Present Continuous ("She is wearing..."), WH-questions ("What is she wearing?", "Where do people wear this?"), Present Simple ("Women wear this dress at festivals."), and vocabulary (attire, traditional clothing, jewelry, celebration, event, wear, beautiful, festive, during, at, in).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice (Listen and Circle):</b> The teacher distributes a worksheet with pictures of different traditional clothing items and events. The teacher plays short audio snippets (e.g., "She is wearing a beautiful dress with a lot of jewelry for the celebration.", "He is wearing traditional clothing at the event.") Students listen and circle the correct picture that matches the audio description.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice (Listen and Complete):</b> The teacher plays a slightly longer audio describing another person's traditional attire. Students have a sentence completion worksheet with blanks for key information (e.g., "Maria is wearing a _____. She is wearing it at the _____. People use it during important _____. It is a very _____ dress."). Students listen and write the missing words from the vocabulary list (e.g., 'traditional clothing', 'event', 'celebrations', 'beautiful'). The teacher pauses the audio for students to write.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Check (Listening & Responding):</b> Students work in pairs. Teacher provides simplified sentence starters (e.g., "What is she wearing? Where do people wear this?"). The teacher plays a new audio description of a person wearing traditional attire. Students listen together, then take turns asking and answering the questions based on what they heard. For example, Student A asks, "What is she wearing?" Student B listens and answers, "She is wearing traditional attire." Teacher monitors for pronunciation, especially /w/ and /t/ sounds, and correct vocabulary usage.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Fluency focus)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative information gap listening activity.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b> Students work in groups of three. Each student receives a picture of a different traditional Panamanian clothing item, along with a few bullet points describing it (e.g., Student A: a pollera, worn at festivals, beautiful jewelry. Student B: a montuno, worn for parades, comfortable. Student C: a diablico sucio costume, worn during carnival, colorful). Students do NOT show their pictures. One student describes their picture orally, focusing on what the person is wearing, when, and where. The other two students listen carefully and draw what they hear, then try to guess the type of traditional clothing. They can ask clarifying WH-questions such as "What is she wearing?" or "Where do people wear this attire?" to ensure they capture details about the attire, time, and location. They switch roles until everyone has had a chance to describe and listen/draw/guess. This activity promotes active listening and encourages students to ask for clarification, aligning with the learning outcome.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the Production stage, focusing on listening comprehension and ability to extract specific details, and a short individual listening quiz.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Individual Listening Quiz. The teacher plays a new, short audio description of someone wearing traditional clothing (e.g., "My cousin, Carlos, is wearing a Montuno at the town fair this weekend. It's a special event. He looks very festive."). Students listen and answer three multiple-choice questions based on the audio, focusing on time, quantity (implied by singular 'a Montuno'), and location.
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What is Carlos wearing? a) A pollera b) A Montuno c) Jewelry</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Where is he wearing it? a) At home b) At a celebration c) At the town fair</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">When is he wearing it? a) During school b) This weekend c) Yesterday</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correctly identifies specific information (time, quantity, location) from the audio.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Demonstrates understanding of key vocabulary related to traditional clothing and events.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can understand when people talk about what they are wearing." (Yes / Almost / No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can hear the difference between 'wearing' and 'these'." (Yes / Almost / No)</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words about traditional clothing did I learn today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy or difficult about listening and understanding the descriptions?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b> The listening activities were effective for introducing new vocabulary and practicing extracting specific information. Students responded well to the visual prompts and short audio snippets. Some students found it challenging to distinguish between 'at' and 'in' for location details, which suggests a need for more focused practice on prepositions. I will prepare additional visual aids for prepositions and incorporate more repetitive listening drills in the next lesson to reinforce these concepts. The pronunciation of /w/ in 'wearing' and /t/ in 'these' needs continued attention.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> The vocabulary and basic descriptions introduced in this listening lesson will be reinforced in Lesson 2, which will focus on reading comprehension using a short text about traditional clothing, featuring the same key vocabulary words and grammar structures.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>